well right now this is kind of where the  hot spot is in terms of the flooding so  it started  all the way in the south really near  that belgium uh border  and the water has risen up and right now  here in vendo and  this is kind of the area where it's most  serious at the moment  i'm also at a red cross location that is  different from where our coordination  center  for all of these red cross activities  was before so even we've had to evacuate  to a different location  because water is rising and we want to  make sure that  at least um you know none of us are  affected by flooding and we can continue  to provide assistance  so where are you going to and how many  people have you had to evacuate  well it's the local authorities who are  in charge of the evacuations and what we  do as the red cross is we support  wherever we can  so if that means like yesterday night we  heard that a  hospital in the region had to be  evacuated he sent over some of our  ambulances to help  with that evacuation um earlier in the  week  we saw that all the way in the south  there were people in a nursing home that  had been there overnight  um stranded on the second floor they had  no contact with the outside world  so the military had to go and get them  out of that situation and then we were  able to provide them with  temporary shelter for the night bring  them to hotels  make sure they're okay not just  physically but also mentally  obviously it's very challenging if  you're stuck for hours  somewhere where there's no electricity  you have no contact with the outside  world  all you know is that there's water  outside and there's no place for you to  go  they had to evacuate an entire hospital  yes they did that out of precaution  because it's close to a river  and at first we were afraid that that  river might have already overflowed last  night  thankfully it did not but all of those  patients have been  relocated to different hospitals all  over the country  and now we're keeping a close eye to  monitor whether that not  you have the available that you need one  would assume that in this sort of a  situation  water drinkable drinking water and food  are going to be an issue yeah so at the  moment we do have the supplies that we  need but there were some areas earlier  in the week where there were small areas  that were cut off from  any supplies so that was the first  priority getting people out of those  situations  so that they were brought to a safe  place but a lot of them  thankfully have already been able to go  back to their homes but this is really  when  um you know the rebuilding starts  because those homes have been flooded  maybe  cars have been uh damaged there's damage  in the homes  so you know they're all the way in the  south they're already  going back to their homes and seeing  what they can do to repair and now we're  moving further up north to make sure  that all of the people  here are at least in physical safety  making sure that they have  shelter that they have food and water  for the night and then hopefully bring  them back to their homes as soon as they  can  and then we have to see what the next  steps are it's it's interesting because  often when we speak  to people from um humanitarian  organizations  who are experiencing things like this is  often in the geographical south  um have you ever in your  career experienced anything like this  before  no i have not obviously the netherlands  is a country that's um  below sea level so we do we are very  used to dealing with butter we have  um systems like um dikes we have systems  around the ocean that kind of prevent  floods from coming in  the really big one was in the previous  century in 1953  the great flood but um in this area in  the south they have experienced flooding  in the 80s and the 90s as well and i've  spoken to some people over the last few  days that are a little bit older  that said you know i was there for those  floods and  they were bad but i've never seen  anything like this in my life  one would assume that they're going to  be a lot of displaced people there  yeah so we did help to evacuate a bunch  of people i spoke to some people earlier  that were evacuated from that  nursing home that i mentioned earlier  they said we're not going to be able to  go home for a few weeks  um you know they were kind of getting  their bearings after they've been  evacuated and  slept on camp beds for the night and  they said okay well we're just happy  we're safe but what happens now  um so most of them have been placed in  hotels for now  um obviously this is also a tourist area  and a lot of the tourists have  either not come or had to come home had  to go home uh so thankfully there are a  lot of spaces there and where where  there aren't we're setting up emergency  shelter  making sure there are camp beds there  are blankets that people have  toiletries water and food um and then  you know as soon as the water sort of  goes back down  um that's where we can start looking at  how soon people can go back home  how how are you looking at this i mean  how soon do you think once those flood  waters recede people are going to be  able to get back  into their homes are you looking at long  term having to look after  these displaced people well what we're  hoping at the moment is that it won't be  that long because what we've already  seen all the way in the south  is that there are a lot of people that  have been able to go home  um so obviously we're hoping that that  can happen as soon as possible but the  thing with water is that it can be very  unpredictable  so we have to continue to monitor the  situation and  see what happens when that water travels  further towards the north  and the west of the country is it still  going to be very high and are we going  to have issues there  or is that water going to have some  place to go in which case you can start  focusing on relocating  the people in the southern area of the  country are you also concerned that  there's more  rain forecast for the weekend naomi  well that's also something that we are  continuing to monitor but at the moment  outside it's sunny it's dry so that's a  good sign  um but that water needs a place to go  and what we're seeing is that  especially in the more urbanized areas  um  getting rid of the water hasn't been the  priority the priority has has been to  get people out and bring them to safety  so if that water stays there and we do  get more rain later in the week  then that could worsen the situation but  obviously we are  hopeful that that's not going to happen
 

